01. EC2 + Docker

A very common setup:

EC2
 |
 Docker
 |
 +-- Container 1
 +-- Container 2
 +-- Container 3

Example:

EC2
 ↓
Docker
 ↓
Spring Boot Container
 ↓
Port 8080

But for container orchestration at scale, you would often consider:

ECS
EKS

rather than manually managing many Docker containers on EC2.



02. EC2 + Jenkins

A common DevOps lab architecture:

Developer
   ↓
GitHub
   ↓
Jenkins
   ↓
Build
   ↓
Docker Image
   ↓
Deploy
   ↓
EC2

Jenkins itself can run on EC2:

EC2
 └── Jenkins

Or Jenkins can run elsewhere and deploy to EC2.



03. EC2 + Terraform

Instead of manually clicking:

AWS Console
 ↓
Launch EC2

Terraform:

main.tf
   ↓
terraform plan
   ↓
terraform apply
   ↓
EC2

Example:

resource "aws_instance" "app" {
  ami           = "ami-xxxxxxxx"
  instance_type = "t3.micro"
}

In real projects, you'd normally also manage:

VPC
Subnet
Security Group
IAM Role
Launch Template
ALB
ASG

with Terraform.




04. EC2 + S3

A common application pattern:

EC2
 ↓
IAM Role
 ↓
S3

Example:

Application needs an artifact:

S3
 └── app.jar

EC2
 ↓
Download app.jar
 ↓
Run application

No hardcoded AWS credentials.




05. EC2 + Systems Manager

AWS Systems Manager (SSM) is very important for modern DevOps.

Instead of:

Laptop
 ↓
SSH
 ↓
EC2

you can use:

AWS Systems Manager
 ↓
EC2

For example, Session Manager can provide shell access without requiring inbound SSH access.

This can reduce the need to expose port 22.



06. EC2 + Secrets Manager

Don't do:

application.properties

DB_PASSWORD=password123

Instead:

EC2
 ↓
IAM Role
 ↓
Secrets Manager
 ↓
Database credentials

Your application retrieves secrets securely.



07. EC2 + Parameter Store

Similarly:

SSM Parameter Store
       ↓
Configuration
       ↓
EC2 Application

Useful for:

Environment variables
Application configuration
Non-secret parameters
Some secrets




08. EC2 Deployment Strategies

You should know these for DevOps interviews.

1.Rolling deployment
A rolling deployment means:

Replace old application instances with new application instances gradually, instead of replacing all instances at once.

It is commonly used with EC2 Auto Scaling Groups, Kubernetes, ECS, etc.

1. Simple Example

Suppose your production application has 6 EC2 instances:

                ALB
                 ↓
       ┌─────────┼─────────┐
       ↓         ↓         ↓
     EC2-1     EC2-2     EC2-3
     v1.0      v1.0      v1.0

       ↓

     EC2-4     EC2-5     EC2-6
     v1.0      v1.0      v1.0

You release v2.0.

Instead of terminating all 6 instances:

❌ EC2-1
❌ EC2-2
❌ EC2-3
❌ EC2-4
❌ EC2-5
❌ EC2-6

you gradually replace them:

Step 1:
v1 v1 v1 v1 v1 v1

Step 2:
v2 v1 v1 v1 v1 v1

Step 3:
v2 v2 v1 v1 v1 v1

Step 4:
v2 v2 v2 v1 v1 v1

...

Final:
v2 v2 v2 v2 v2 v2

That's a rolling deployment.

Deploy

EC2-1 → new
EC2-2 → new
EC2-3 → new

2. Blue/Green
Blue
 ├── EC2
 ├── EC2
 └── EC2

Green
 ├── EC2
 ├── EC2
 └── EC2

Traffic switches:

Blue
 ↓
Green


3. Canary

Small percentage first:

95% → old
5%  → new

Then gradually increase if the deployment is healthy.



Golden AMI

Very common DevOps concept.

Instead of configuring every server after launch:

Launch EC2
 ↓
Install everything
 ↓
Configure everything

Create a standardized image:


Golden AMI
 ├── OS
 ├── Security configuration
 ├── Docker
 ├── Monitoring agent
 └── Required dependencies

Then:

Golden AMI
    ↓
ASG
 ↓ ↓ ↓
EC2 EC2 EC2
